-The original 16-trial preliminary campaign supports a limited premise: scheduled scaling produced different completion/resource trade-offs across the tested conditions. They do not show that waiting is preferable during short overload, that targeted reassignment is effective, or that the complete controller improves on existing policies. Initial hot-partition placement differs systematically in the concentrated-input comparisons, so those results do not isolate consumer-count effects. Subsequent action comparisons must control or deliberately cross placement before their results are used to evaluate a selector. Those original trials do not evaluate hot-key splitting, combined actions, or the complete controller.
+The 24 completed performance trials in Section~5 support a limited premise: scheduled scaling produced different completion/resource trade-offs across the tested conditions. The original 16-trial campaign retained initial-placement confounding in its concentrated-input comparisons. Eight later trials verified matching starting conditions and repeated two skew cases: scaling lowered completion p99 in both 80/20 pairs, while single-partition outcomes were mixed. Placement of added consumers and changing shared-node contention remained uncontrolled. These observations motivate the proposed decision study; they do not show that waiting is preferable during short overload, that targeted reassignment or splitting is effective, or that the complete controller improves on existing policies. None of the 24 trials evaluates hot-key splitting, combined actions, or the complete controller.
